# Statistics & Probability Standards

## Prefer Bayesian when beliefs update

- When new outcomes arrive sequentially, update beliefs with **Bayesian updating** (priors → likelihood → posterior). Do not restart from a fresh frequentist point each time unless the process truly reset.
- State prior source (empirical, elicited, weakly informative) and why it is appropriate.
- Report posterior summaries with intervals (credible intervals), not only MAP/mean.

## Sample size & power

- Before claiming a signal, check whether the sample can detect an effect of the claimed size.
- Small-n → widen uncertainty; do not overfit narratives to noise.
- Multiple comparisons / many tickers / many features → adjust or pre-register the hypothesis; otherwise discount.

## Distributions over point myths

- Default deliverable is a **distribution or scenario set**, not a single “expected” number.
- Fat tails: for returns, losses, and rare events, prefer heavy-tailed or mixture models over naive Normal when evidence supports it.
- Dependence matters: correlate shocks when assets/share factors; independence is an assumption you must justify.

## Calibration language

- Use probability language that is auditable: “P(loss > X) ≈ … under model M with assumptions A.”
- Distinguish **aleatory** uncertainty (inherent randomness) from **epistemic** uncertainty (model/data ignorance).
- Never equate model confidence with real-world certainty.

## Forbidden shortcuts

- No p-hacking narratives (“significant after we sliced three ways”).
- No silent winsorizing / outlier deletion without stating the rule and sensitivity.
- No treating backtest Sharpe as out-of-sample truth.
